--- HOW TO USE (not part of the diff) -------------------------------------------------------
Apply to third_party/virglrenderer (a clone of liminavm/virglrenderer at the manifest rev),
rebuild with scripts/build-virglrenderer.sh, and boot with LIMINA_GPU_MEM_BUDGET_MIB=2048.

    git -C third_party/virglrenderer apply ../../spikes/venus-churn-retention/fault-inject-paths-3-4.patch
    ./scripts/build-virglrenderer.sh
    ./crates/limina-vmm/sign.sh

Both injectors are OFF unless a file exists ON THE HOST — a file rather than an env var so RED
and GREEN run against ONE boot (a worker's environment is fixed at exec, and rebooting between
arms puts a fresh guest, fresh contexts and a fresh census between two numbers that only mean
anything compared to each other):

    /tmp/limina-fault-leak-errpath        path 3 RED — leak the pre-create IOSurface on the
                                          error path instead of freeing it
    /tmp/limina-fault-fatal-skip-release  path 4 RED — drop the release of an imported client
                                          IOSurface at teardown

testcomp/teardown-matrix.sh path3 / path4 drive both arms and touch these itself. REMEMBER THE
FILES ARE HOST-SIDE: touching them over ssh puts them in the guest, where nothing reads them,
and the RED then runs as a second GREEN — a broken arm that looks like a clean result. Both
arms print an injection count for exactly that reason.

The path-3 injector fires ONLY for creates carrying the vehicle's private doomed modifier
(0x00fedeadbeef0123, from limina-testcomp's `badmod`), so arming it adds no allocation to any
other traffic.

An earlier path-4 injector skipped the whole instance sweep in vkr_context_destroy and SIGSEGV'd
the worker: the object table is destroyed immediately after, and freeing objects whose device and
instance are already gone is a use-after-free. A fault injector that crashes the process proves
nothing about retention — which is why this one drops exactly the reference under test and leaves
the object graph intact.
